We are a third-party software company that works with almost all the 
Unix vendors.  Our experience is nothing like what you are describing.  
If anything, DEC has one of the strongest third-party program in the market. 
They make seed machines (loaners) available for a large number of vendors, have
porting centers all over the world, provide deeply discounted systems for
purchase by developers, and operate a world-wide network for use by their
partners.  

I can not understand the kinds of problems your suppliers are having,
particularly StatSci.  (I know SAS has plenty of Alphas.)  I suggest that
vendors that want to work with DEC call 1-800-DEC-ISVN (in the US -- Call 
your local office outside the US).  

We received a loaner machine for porting so that we could be part of the
initial Alpha announcements in 1992 and later replaced the loaner with a
purchased system.  DEC has also been good about making early versions of
software available for testing.

For the record, while a loaner machine is adequate for a test port of a 
product, your software supplier will need to invest in buying at least one
of each supported hardware platform family.  This investment for the Alpha 
is probably $5k to $10k, depending on options.
