In an earlier posting on this subject, I made a terrible mistake.  I
had thought that I had a compiler warning with DEC's cxx compiler that
was telling me that in a comparision (<) of two 64-bit pointers, the
pointers were being coerced to 32-bit integers before the comparison
was made.  This had me worried.
	I discovered today a terrible error on my part.  Ridiculous as
this may seem, I was looking at the correct line number, but the wrong
file.  I was looking at the header file rather than the .cc file.  It
was an unfortunate co-incidence that the header file had, on the same
line, an inline function with the same pointers involved.  In fact,
the warning was due to a coercion of a pointer to `int', which was
quickly fixed by changing the coercion to `long'.
	My apologies for my spurious post.
							Tony.
