> will now be spread across two filesystems called /space2 and /space3; users
> in one group (and I don't mean "group" in the Unix sense) will have their
> files on /space2 but will be prevented (by quotas) from keeping anything on
> /space3, and vice versa for users in the other group.  (The reason is a
> political/management one that isn't important here.)
> 
> Users in both groups have previously generated files which make reference
> to full path names under the old system (eg, /img/bland/ct/smith/head.fld).
>  It would be nice if these same paths would be recognized under the new
> system for _all_ users.  However, I don't see how to do this since /img is
> now spread across /space2 and /space3.  Otherwise a simple link called /img
> that would resolve to the desired place would work.  But for one group the
> link needs to resolve to /space2 and /space3 for the other.

Thanks to you who responded.  The solution is fairly simple - at least for
this specific case:  Just create /img and within it make a series of
symbolic links that point to the appropriate filesystem/directory for each
user:

  mkdir /img; cd /img
  ln -s /space2/data/smith smith
  ln -s /space3/data/jones jones
  ... and so on...

Thanks again, Peyton

