I reported earlier an apparent failure of a switch construct with
DEC's `c89' compiler.  It appeared that the `case -1 :' branch of the
switch was not taken, and c89 took the `default :' branch instead.
This did not happen using the `cc' compiler supplied with OSF/1.3.
	What I had overlooked, as was pointed out to me be a reply,
was the type coercion of the function returning the value for the
switch construct, something like

sign f(void) { return -1; }

switch(f()) {
  ...
}

to simplify things greatly.  I forgot, in my earlier post, to describe
the type `sign'.  It turned out that this was an unsigned int, a
mistake in the declaration of sign (which should be an int for my
application).  Once this declaration of `sign' was changed, the
program compiled correctly under c89.  I haven't yet had the chance to
isolate where the coercion of -1 to an unsigned value causes the
failure (though of course, no compiler should be expected to guarantee
that it *wouldn't* fail), or in particular to find where cc and c89
differ in their interpretation of such a coercion.
	In any case, to summarise, there was no problem with c89 when
I stopped trying to coerce negative values into unsigned values.
