Thought I'd relate some of our experiences so that someone else
might avoid a pitfall (or two) we encountered.  In summary,
be careful if (1), you try to install your root directory onto
an RZ24 or (2), you are trying an upgrade install using a
3rd party disk drive.

First, we tried the update route on a machine that runs with a
single RZ56 disk.  After about 20+ minutes, installupdate came back
and said that it wouldn't continue because /usr/local and
/usr/var/spool/mail weren't directories.  They were symbolic links,
so we just created directories in their place and tried again.
After about 30+ minutes, installupdate says we don't have enough
disk space for our selected subsets.  So, we remove a few subsets and
wait a long time again.  Still no room.  At the point, we opt for
the full install.  No problems.

Then, we go to another Alpha (DEC 3000/300).  When we originally installed
DEC OSF/1 V1.3 on this machine, it had a Seagate ST11200N as its only
disk drive.  At that time, we fooled around with disklabel and installed
DEC OSF/1 V1.3 on the Seagate with no problem.  This has worked well
for several months now.

We had planned to attempt an update (the ST11200N is bigger than a RZ56),
so we loaded up the CD-ROM and installed the new firmware.  After that
the existing DEC OSF/1 would no longer boot off the Seagate drive.
We abandoned (again) the update strategy.

This particular machine now has a DEC RZ24.  The installation
guide and/or release notes say that the RZ24 won't hold a complete
system (/, /usr, and swap), but that you can put the root and swap
on the RZ24 and /usr elsewhere.  After our experience with booting
off the Seagate, we decide to pursue that option.

We're going through the installation and we choose the RZ24
as the system disk.  The installation program comes back and
says something like
	RZ24 not big enough for all the stuff you need,
	choose the advanced installation.
We're encouraged by this message, and of course we follow the advice.
A root file system is installed on the RZ24 and we're asked to change
the boot variables and reboot to continue the installation.

We do.

The installation continues after the reboot and prints a
message "Default file system cannot be used" and then
immediately (with no chance for interaction) starts to make
a file system for /usr on the g partition of the RZ24.
Now, it doesn't take long to do a newfs on the 8Mbytes in
the g partition of the RZ24 and, of course, soon after it
starts to install /usr within these 8Mbytes, the disk space
is exhausted.  However, the install does try again, saying
	"Another attempt will be made to install software subsets."
That has the same results.

We try once one and get the same results.  On the second attempt
we are very careful about what we type.

On the third attempt, we install the system onto the Seagate.
This time, there are no problems.  The system boots nicely
and seems to be running quite well.


