In article <GRUNWALD.94Mar15205504@foobar.cs.colorado.edu> grunwald@foobar.cs.colorado.edu writes:
>create a memory file system using `mfs' and then mount it on something
>you don't use. This removes the memory from circulation (at boot time)
>but you don't need to remove it physically.

I don't believe that this guarantees that you won't be able to use that
real memory. An mfs filesystem exists in virtual memory, and it's memory
can be paged, so a 32meg mfs filesystem isn't necessarily occupying
32meg of _real_ memory.
