We just received the DEC AXP (Alpha) c89 compiler as part of DEC's cxx
C++ compiler package.  We are running OSF/1.3.  I tried recompiling an
old program which compiled under cc and ran correctly.  However, after
compilation with c89, the program failed.  Debugging with dbx, and
with the aid of a few printf statements which I had to add, I found
that, when compiled with c89, a switch statement of the form

	switch(<function evaluation>) {
	case -1: <blah>; break;
	case  1: <blah>; break;
	default: <blah>
        }

was reaching the default statement, even though the function
evaluation yielded -1.  The same statement selected the -1 case when
compiled with cc.
	As such, I would recommend cc over c89 if possible, in spite
of the fact that the pre-processor for cc is not proper ANSI (can't
leave spaces in from of #line directives, for example).
					Cheers,
							Tony.
