	(a) Put BackSpace and Delete on the same key, and use the "Shift"
	    keys to distinguish.  Works for terminal app's, but doesn't
	    work with the HP & Sun app's -- they see "<shift><Delete>"
	    not "BackSpace".  Besides - yick!

	(b) Remap the "Delete" key to produce "BackSpace".  Put "Delete"
	    function on, say, the "Select" key.  But VMS logins will
	    require use of the "Select" key, not the "Delete(now BackSpace)"
	    key.

	(c) Remap the "Delete" key to produce "BackSpace".  Eliminate VMS.
	    Live without "forward-delete" functionality in a small number
	    of applications.  But we can't quite get rid of VMS yet.

	(d) Remap some other key to be the "BackSpace" key.  Then the app's
	    we want to run are a pain to use.

4. Quit buying Alpha's and buy Sun's & HP's instead.  But OSF/1 on Alpha's
   are otherwise a wonderful combination.


  In short, though DEC has been steadfast in not reshaping the keyboard
every few years, as Sun and HP have, this is now being a pain.  Last I
knew, there was not a Motif version of WordPerfect for OSF/1 on the Alpha,
and Island Graphics has said that they don't intend to port to the Alpha
under OSF/1.  They both count on the existance and use of the BackSpace
key.  These are but two applications, but I think they are representative
of the stance in the marketplace.  It's nice to use a standard, but DEC's
competitors won't use it.

  Perhaps as more applications are ported to the OSF/1 Alpha, this problem
will work itself out.  But for now, I think the best option is #3(b) --
unless you know how to solve #2.  What do you think?  Is this a problem
in your arena?  What do you think DEC's stance ought to be regarding the
ANSI keyboard?  [For this last question, I'm hoping DEC will refrain from
replying.]

Thanks...

Don

