Well, I didn't provide the actual case, because I thought it too long,
but I will now.  The function returns a `signof' value - if this isn't
an integer, then I'll be amused.  The evalutaion is a bit compicated,
but runs as follows.  The switch that fails looks like

    switch(key_compare(p_tbky,p_tb->ca_p_parent->p_key))
      {
       case -1: p_tb->ca_p_parent->p_left =p_new; break;
       case  1: p_tb->ca_p_parent->p_right=p_new; break;
       default: as_bomb("tb_ins: Key comparison function is inconsistent.");
      }
 
and runs through to the default case when the function returns -1
(only under c89, not under cc.  It works under cc).

keycompare is a macro, defined as follows:

#define key_compare(a,b) ((*(p_tb->p_kycm))((a),(b)))

The particular function `p_kycm' which is called in the failing case
is the following:

LOCAL sign cm_name P_((name_t *,name_t *));
LOCAL sign cm_name(name1,name2)
/* Compares two names and returns:               */
/*    -1 if name1 < name2                        */
/*     0 if name1 = name2                        */
/*     1 if name1 > name2                        */
/* Provides a full ordering on the set of names. */
name_t *name1;
name_t *name2;
{
 return signof(strcmp(&(*name1)[0],&(*name2)[0]));
}

where the P_ macro just serves here to create an ANSI prototype,
and the name_t structure is a fixed length array of characters.

	As I noted, this fails when compiled under c89, not cc, on a
DEC AXP running OSF/1.3.  I haven't attempted to try to convert this
to a trivial example to check under what conditions it can occur,
because changing back from c89 to cc is less effort for me than trying
to create an example where it fails.  Given that switch statements
usually work, I can only hypothesise that this is a bug which doesn't
arise in simple test programs, only in larger programs.
