In article<2ma4bd$d5c@chnews.intel.com>
tlevie@sedona.intel.com (Ted Levie~) writes:

| In article <thadCMt7Lz.BuC@netcom.com>, thad@netcom.com (Thad Floryan) writes:
| [deletia]
| > If you have "work that needs to be done on the machine (proxesses [sic]
| > running for weeks)", what do you do about power failuers?  For that reason
| > alone, your programs/software should do checkpoints so it can be restarted at
| > the checkpoint after unscheduled service interruptions.  Surely you don't
| > just restart a weeks-long run from the beginning every time!  Or do you?
| > 
| If you've got stuff that must run, uninterrupted for weeks, you should have
| more than checkpoints going on:
| 
| 1) UPS UPS UPS.  power failures should NOT be an issue if you have critical
| systems.
| 
| 2) Live backups can be done handily with mirroring software.  That is, mirror
| your disk to another so that they're written to identically.  "Break the
| mirror", so that you're no longer mirroring.  Backup the mirror disk.  Not
| necessarily an inexpensive way to backup a home system, but in a mission-
| critical environment, I would expect something like this to be in place.

You'll find no argument from me about those two points.  ALL my systems are
on UPS, and I have a 4KW generator for use when necessary.  If you remember
the Loma Prieta ("SF") Earthquake of 1989, you may remember my article in
ca.earthquakes how my systems were operating DURING and AFTER the 'quake.

Most my clients have installed UPS systems at my recommendation.

My comment re: ``If you have "work that needs to be done on the machine
(proxesses [sic]  running for weeks)", what do you do about power failuers?''
was in response to the original poster who claimed inability to do standalone
dumps because of the long-running processes on his system.  His disk(s) could
still go belly-up during his computation runs irrespective of backup UPS power.

Thad Floryan [ thad@btr.com, thad@cup.portal.com, thad@netcom.com ]


