merritt@provolone.bchem.washington.edu (Ethan A Merritt) writes:
|In article <thadCMt7Lz.BuC@netcom.com> thad@netcom.com (Thad Floryan) writes:
|>[...]
|>Right (re: opinion).  So let's set some facts straight.
|>
|>VMS arrived on the scene in 1978, so it hasn't been 20 years.  VMS' file
|>system has an entry for date/time of file backup.  VMS' file system does NOT
|>have an entry for file last access date/time (this is a serious blunder (at
|>least including everying up to but not including VMS 6.*))
|
|	Not true. VMS has a last-modification date in the file header.
|	Also, with ACLs you can timestamp any sort of access you like.
|	When using VMS Backup over the net to backup a unix file system
|	(as I recommend below) the unix modification bit is used.

WHAT is not true?  VMS does _NOT_ have a file ACCESS date/time (i.e. time of
last read).  This means you cannot determine when a file was surreptitiously
read by someone else (or how long a file has been essentially "inactive").

At least nothing is documented "obviously" for VMS.  If you know of a way to
access such information, would you please email me how ACLs can be used to
record/track file _READ_ date/times?  One gross deficiency of VMS docs is the
lack of a "SEE ALSO" (a la UNIX) referencing related material.  Thanks!

|>UNIX' dump(8) in level 0 is analogous to VMS' image disk backup (which _must_
|>be run in "single-user" [standalone] mode).
|
|	Also not true, except for the system disk.

Many UNIX systems only have one disk.  So my _analogy_ is correct for the
system (i.e. "root") disk.

|Add me to the list of folks totally unimpressed by any of the standard
|unix backup tools.  I make level 0 dumps of the root partitions whenever
|the kernal is changed, or the system is updated.  Beyond that I do all
|backups, incremental or otherwise, using the VMS Backup utility over the
|network.  I've been doing this for 3-4 years now, ever since getting
|burned badly by relying on tar+dump tapes, and have never encountered any
|problems other than an annoying one listed below.  This does, of course,
|require a VMS machine with a tape drive somewhere on the net...

And proper network connectivity to your UNIX systems.  I have TGV's software
on my VAXen; much better than any of the crap from DEC.  I only use it for
rcp, telnet, ftp, and print spooling from the VAXen to the UNIX boxes.

And, I fully agree with you about backups.  Given the dependence placed on
UNIX by so many companies and governments, I'm simply astonished at the dearth
of reasonable VENDOR-SUPPLIED backup tools and backup cognizance in the
filesystem (my original complaint! :)   There are 3rd party solutions, but
this sidesteps the issue that backup facilities are so important they must be
supplied Part'N'Parcel with the system as shipped from the vendor.

Thad Floryan [ thad@btr.com, thad@cup.portal.com, thad@netcom.com ]


