I recently did the bulk of the work in porting my company's product to
DEC OSF/1 on Alpha AXP.  This was our first 64-bit architecture.
Here's what I determined about the behaviour of the XDR routines on DEC
OSF/1 V1.3:

(a) xdr_{u_,}long in user space takes a pointer to a long.  It encodes/decodes
from/to that long.

If it's encoding, it range-checks the value.
	* If xdr_long, the top 33 bits must be all zeros or ones.  (if
	this is not the case, the value will lose precision when stuffed
	into a 32-bit XDR encoding).
	* If xdr_u_long, the top 32 bits must be clear.
        * practical import:
	  (i) do not xdr_long or xdr_u_long pointers. They won't fit.
	  (ii) don't xdr_long or xdr_u_long uninitialized or otherwise
	  garbage long/u_long values (e.g. random numbers).  They
	  probably won't fit.
	  (iii) You will get an XDR failure if the range check fails, so
	  it's fairly simple to find the offending out-of-range item.

If it's decoding, it zero-fills (u_long) or sign-extends (long) the top
32 bits.

(b) xdr_{u_,}long in the kernel take pointers to int (in V1.3, at
least).  If you have longs in kernel RPC code, you'll need to roll your
own xdr_*long routines to do the range-checking and zero/sign extending.
--
John Kohl <jtk@atria.com> or <jtkohl@mit.edu>
working for but not representing:	Atria Software
