root@belvedere.sbay.org (David E. Fox) writes:

>I recommend using tar for full backups, and cpio for incremental backups,
>because cpio accepts a list of filenames on stdin that it will backup. This
>allows one to put a time-stamp file somewhere (like /etc/backup) and a
>find / -newer /etc/backup would give all files newer than the time-stamp file,
>which you'd pipe into cpio.

We have used tar in the past (now use BackupEDGE/CTAR) with xargs to take
a list of files from the standard input.  Works well.  We do several
levels of backup (all with the same tool).

1.  Monthlys - Complete full sys of everything.  2 copies.  one goes
    offsite, the other into a fireproof cabinet (data level) insite.
    These are done on the first Monday of the month.  These have to
    be done manually since they go to >1 volume.  Twelve tape sets
    in the offsite box rotated yearly, three sets onsite rotated
    every three months.

2.  Weeklys.  These are an incremental (or differential, never figured
    out the differenc).  We use find -newer to get files created or
    modified since the last monthly backup.  These are done by cron
    at 4:30 am saturdays.  Five tape sets rotated each month (of
    course, we usually don't go to five weeks).

3,  Dailys.  Same method as weeklys except they get anything new or changed
    since the last weekly.  These are done at 4:30 am on Tuesday-Thursday.
    Four tapes rotated weekly.

This method has the advantage of never needing more than three tapes to
completely restore a system.  This also covers us in case we need to restore
a version of a file "as of" a particulr date in the last year.
