Subject: Re: Problems with semaphores and shared memory segments
Message-ID: <rang.763228481@alpha.ipk.fhg.de>
Date: 9 Mar 94 15:54:41 GMT
References: <2l5433$i11@explorer.clark.net>
Sender: usenet@sunplt.ipk.fhg.de
Organization: IPK-Berlin, Fraunhofer Gesellschaft, Germany
Lines: 24

jcp@clark.net (Jody C. Patilla) writes:

>We have observed a problem in using semaphores and shared memory segments,
>in that the semaphores are hanging around after the process using them 
>dies - the system is not cleaning up after itself. It is our understanding
>that the semaphore should only remain as long as there is a process open
>for writing against it. Is this a bug, or are we missing something here?

No, this is not a bug. The System V semantics of message queues, shared
memory and semaphores explicitely state that they remain after the
process exits, normally or abnormally.

Personally , I don't like this behaviour. For instance, I find OS/2's
queue management much easier to use because there, queues are
automagically removed when the owning process dies.




