In article <wkeil.761437283@sevior>, wkeil@sevior.triumf.ca (Werner Keil) writes:
>             we are having problems with the cpu usage of low priority processes
> on our Alphas and I was wondering if anybody else has observed this kind of behavior,
> or, even better, knows a fix. Here is the problem in a nutshell:
> 
> Platforms: AXP 3000/400, OSF/1 V1.3 rev. 111; Decstation 5000/240, Ultrix 4.3.
>   (...)
>   On Ultrix, the low prio process will consume less than 1% (0.5% on average),
> but on OSF/1 it will consistently get 20% and more! Here is a sample ps listing:

We have the same problem here, with almost the same AXP configuration:
AXP 3000/400, OSF/1 V1.3A rev. 112. Our system manager reported it to
Digital about a month ago, I think, but I haven't heard about any answer yet.

The problem is unambiguously seen with this 1-line C program loop.c:
    main() { for(;;); }
started twice: at normal priority and at a very low one, using /usr/bin/nice
(not tcsh's built-in nice) and/or /usr/sbin/renice, and checking status with
ps (and top):

%CPU STAT  NI PRI UPR PPR PAGEIN INBLK OUBLK      TIME COMMAND
26.0 R N   19  63  63   0      0     0     3   0:19.65 loop
72.0 R      0  59  44   4      0     0     0   0:54.83 loop
 0.0 R      0  44  44  19      0     0     1   0:00.09 ps -o pcpu,state,nice,pr

> So, what gives? Is nice/renice still broken in V1.3? Is scheduling in OSF so
> different from BSD that this is actually correct behavior (hard to believe)?
> Is it just some kernel parameters that need adjusting?  
> 
> Any hints would be greatly appreciated (including RTFM, as long as you tell me
> which FM).

The nice/renice binary programs do not seem broken in themselves, since they
correctly alter the priority value / niceness of the process.
The _scheduling in OSF_ is a good guess at the problem.
Hopefully some kernel parameters can adjust this scheduling permanently.

The manual pages for sched_getscheduler(3) & sched_setscheduler(3) may give some
hints. They mention three different scheduling policies:
  +  SCHED_FIFO - specifies the first-in, first-out (FIFO) scheduling policy
  +  SCHED_RR - specifies the round-robin scheduling policy
  +  SCHED_OTHER - specifies the standard timesharing scheduling policy
where user processes apparently use the latter, so that
  "the scheduler adjusts process priorities. Recalculation of process
   priorities results in preemption."
I tried to test sched_getscheduler(3) today, but the linker couldn't find
it. A search with nm(1) of /lib/lib*.a and /usr/lib/lib*.a was unsuccesful.
(Only the superuser can call sched_setscheduler).

