GaRY NEwEll <newell@simpson.edu> wrote:
>
> OK - dumb question. I'm trying to get a lab full of macs to be able to
>run X clients off of my Alpha - nothing fancy just a few window driven
>applications. 

>Has anyone managed to get this working out
>there? If so do you remember which files needed to be adjusted on the
>Alpha and more improtantly do you have any suggestions as to what
>command I should have students enter in order to connect and start a
>session right off the bat? 

Yes, using MacX version 1.2.  This requires no changes at all on the Alpha,
it is entirely a matter of setting up MacX (and MacTCP if you haven't already
done so).  The following should get you started:

Open MacX and choose "new command" under the "remote" menu.

In the Remote Command box enter: /usr/bin/X11/xterm -ls -display "(r)display"
  where (r) is a special character generated by holding down the option key
  when pressing r.

For Command name enter whatever you want to call it.

Display should be rootless, either color of B&W depending

Output  your preference..I use save

For your case of multiple people using the same command set, leave
Username and Password blank, but choose Execute at startup.  MacX will
prompt for the user's login name and password before connecting to the 
remote system.

Click the (Host) button, enter the name or address of the desired host,
and click on (OK).

Click on (Execute) and see how it goes...

Once set up, save the command set to file and put it on the desktop or
someplace easy to find.  When double-clicked, it will open MacX and 
initiate the connection process.






