In article <2loa0b$sqk@vixen.cso.uiuc.edu> robm@ncsa.uiuc.edu (Rob McCool) writes:


Rob> Hi, in the source for my information server, I need to find out what port a
Rob> given socket is attached to while running under inetd. I'm finding that
Rob> while my code to do this works elsewhere, it fails miserably under OSF/1,
Rob> returning a very large integer (usually 20480 or 36895). Here is the code:

DEC OSF/1 on Alpha AXP uses a different byte order from network byte
order; getsockname() reports the address and port number in network byte
order.

You should use ntohs() (network to host short) to convert the value.  On
machines which have matching network and host byte order, the function
is a null macro; on machines like the DEC Alpha AXP, it's a
byte-swapping function.
