I am getting extremely frustrated trying to run the FUSE call graph
browser on a  16000 line C program in 20-ish source files.  Since
it would be a right pain to report bugs with this kind of program
I thought I would first ask here whether anyone else has had the same
problems.

1) After some manipulation including the use of "expand all" to see
   all the routines, followed by restriction (by means of "ignore" or
   only displaying routines connected to "main") the module and file
   names displayed in the boxes become corrupted.  That is, instead of
   "myprog.c" or "input" I get random text or transmission line noise.
   The only way to recover is by exiting the program - none of the
   restart options help.

2) The "print" option under the "graph" menu fails to work except for
   the first few couple of times it is used in a session.  The message
   "m4:-:1 can't open file" is printed on stderr.  After a bit of 
   investigation using "trace" I have established that
   a) The program attemts to do a "system("m4 <some-file> > <out-file>");
      immediately followed by an "unlink("some-file");".  The
      "system()" call is exiting prematurely and the file is unlinked
      before m4 can process it.  [ I am guessing that the code calls
      "system()" rather than doing the fork and exec itself.]
   b) The "trace" command reveals that the "wait4()" is returning a
      *different* (in fact lower) pid than the immediately preceding
      "fork()".  When the print works it returns the same pid, as I
      understand it should.

   could this be a bug in the OS or libraries?  Or is it just another
   manifestation of stack corruption?
 

I am running OSF/1 1.3A and FUSE Version 1.2 on a 3000/800.
